ames_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/41"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "200 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "3 –  4 August 2021"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2106"} "Japan National Stadium, Kasumigaoka, Shinjuku, Tokyo"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19000379"} "48 from 33 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/105512"} "Usain Bolt"] " had won the last three gold medals in this event, and had competed in the Olympic 200 metres since 2004. In his absence the favorite was American " [:a {:href "/athletes/2104512"} "Noah Lyles"] ", who had won the 2019 World Championships. His biggest challengers were expected to be Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/133005"} "Andre De Grasse"] ", the silver medalist behind Bolt in Rio, another American, " [:a {:href "/athletes/147336"} "Kenny Bednarek"] ", and Liberian " [:a {:href "/athletes/145127"} "Joseph Fahnbulleh"] ", who was raised in the United States, competed for the University of Florida, and had won the NCAA title earlier in the year."] "\n\n" [:p {} "All of the favorites made it through to the final, although the second semi-final was absurdly close, with Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/126061"} "Aaron Brown"] " winning in 19.982, with Fahnbulleh second with the same time, and Lyles third in 19.983, meaning he only advanced as a time qualifier. The first semi-final was won by American teenager " [:a {:href "/athletes/147326"} "Erriyon Knighton"] ", while De Grasse led the third semi-final."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the final De Grasse got out well and led the field coming off the turn, but he was quickly caught by Lyles and Bednarek took a narrow lead at 150 metres. It was De Grasse, however, who was strongest in the last 50 metres and he finished in a Canadian record of 19.62 for the gold medal. Behind him the Americans went 2-3-4, with Bednarek running 19.68 for the silver medal, and Lyles finishing in 19.74 for bronze, as Knighton finished ahead of Fahnbulleh in fourth place."] "\n\n" [:p {} "For De Grasse this was redemption as he had struggled through multiple injuries and sub-par seasons since his Rio exploits. He had missed the 2017 World Championships with hamstring issues, and then re-injured the muscle in 2018, as the year postponement to 2021 aided his recovery."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "1425"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/133005"} "Andre De Grasse"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]] [:td {} "20.56 (3 h3)"] [:td {} "19.73 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "19.62 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3905"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/147336"} "Kenny Bednarek"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "20.01 (1 h6)"] [:td {} "19.83 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "19.68 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3950"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2104512"} "Noah Lyles"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "20.18 (1 h7)"] [:td {} "19.99 (3 h2)"] [: